-- title:
--   Descent of an eigensystem from level Nr to level N
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field, let $p$ be a prime with $p$ in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}$, and let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k = \mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal{O}$, that is, a two-dimensional $k$-vector space $\bar\rho.V$ together with a multiplicative map $\bar\rho.\rho$ from $\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $\mathrm{End}_k(\bar\rho.V)$ that is trivial on the automorphisms fixing some finite-dimensional intermediate field; assume $\bar\rho$ is absolutely irreducible, i.e. its base change to $\overline{k}$ has no Galois-stable submodule other than $\bot$ and $\top$. Let $r \ne p$ be a prime such that for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $r$ in its nonunits and every $\sigma$ lying in the decomposition subgroup of $P$ and acting on the residue field of $P$ by $x \mapsto x^{r}$, one has $\mathrm{tr}\,\bar\rho.\rho(\sigma)^2 \ne (r+1)^2$ in $k$. Let $N \ne 0$ with $Nr \ne 0$ and $r \nmid N$, and let $S$ be a finite set of naturals containing every prime divisor of $Nr$. Write $\mathbb{T}^S(M)$ for [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra M 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of weight-two cusp forms for $\Gamma_0(M)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid M$, $\ell \notin S$, and $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, $q \notin S$. Let $\theta_N \colon \mathbb{T}^S(Nr) \to k$ be a ring homomorphism such that for every prime $\ell \nmid Nr$ with $\ell \notin S$ and every Frobenius $\sigma$ as above at $\ell$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho.\rho(\sigma)$ equals $X^2 - \theta_N(T_\ell)X + \ell$. Let $\mathcal{O}'$ be another characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field which is a module-finite $\mathcal{O}$-algebra along a local homomorphism, and let $\chi' \colon \mathbb{T}^S(Nr) \to \mathcal{O}'$ be a ring homomorphism reducing modulo the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}'$ to $\theta_N$ composed with the induced map of residue fields. Then there exists a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain $\mathcal{O}''$ with finite residue field, module-finite over $\mathcal{O}'$ along a local and injective structure map, and a ring homomorphism $\chi \colon \mathbb{T}^S(N) \to \mathcal{O}''$ such that for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid Nr$, $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \notin S$, the value $\chi(T_\ell)$ at level $N$ is the image in $\mathcal{O}''$ of $\chi'(T_\ell)$ at level $Nr$.
--
--   This is the converse (easy) direction of Ribet's level-raising criterion in eigensystem form: if the trace condition $\mathrm{tr}\,\bar\rho(\mathrm{Frob}_r)^2 \ne (r+1)^2$ holds, no eigensystem at level $Nr$ congruent to $\bar\rho$ can be genuinely new at $r$, so it already comes from level $N$ after a finite extension of coefficients. It is the arithmetic ingredient in the comparison of the anemic Hecke algebras of levels $N$ and $Nr$ localised at the maximal ideal attached to $\bar\rho$, used when auxiliary primes are introduced in the Taylor–Wiles argument, and it feeds the vanishing statements for the auxiliary-level cohomology carriers.
